note: |
  ## Triage and Analysis

  ### What triggered this alert
  A curl command downloaded a file directly into a temporary
  directory on a Linux host indicating a possible tool transfer
  or payload staging attempt by an attacker.

  ### Possible investigation steps
  - Examine the full process.command_line to identify the URL
    being downloaded and the exact destination file path
  - Check whether the downloaded file was subsequently executed
    by looking for exec events from /tmp /var/tmp or /dev/shm
    in the minutes following this alert
  - Identify the parent process that spawned curl to determine
    if this was triggered by a shell, web server, or unknown binary
  - Investigate outbound network connections from the host to
    the download URL to determine if the server is known malicious
  - Check if the same URL or IP has been seen across other hosts
    in the environment indicating a wider campaign

  ### False positive analysis
  - Legitimate software installers that download components
    to /tmp as part of a standard installation workflow
  - Developer scripts and CI/CD pipelines that pull build
    artifacts or dependencies into temporary directories

  ### Response and remediation
  - Contain: isolate the host immediately if the downloaded
    file was executed or if the source URL is known malicious
  - Collect: preserve the downloaded file before it is deleted
    capture process tree network connections and shell history
  - Eradicate: delete the downloaded payload remove any
    persistence mechanisms installed by the file
  - Recover: reimage if execution is confirmed rotate all
    credentials accessible from the compromised host
  - Escalate immediately if the download was followed by
    execution privilege escalation or outbound C2 connections

  ### References
  - https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1105/
  - https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/004/

false_positives:
